#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SubagentStart: the code-writing and reviewing agents are subagents, so the session-start
# injection never reaches them. This puts the comment law and the laziness ladder in their context
# without waiting for them to read rules/core.md themselves.
set -uo pipefail

input="$(cat)"
agent="$(echo "$input" | jq -r '.agent_type // ""')"

case "$agent" in
  *backend*|*ui*|*frontend-logic*|*feature-builder*|*bug-fixer*|*integrations*|*data-engineer*|\
  *infra*|*code-cleanup*|*reviewer*|*verifier*|*tester*|*e2e*|*perf*|*devops*|*sre*) ;;
  *) exit 0 ;;
esac

read -r -d '' context <<'CONTEXT'
Polaris standard, the two rules that decide whether this code is accepted:

1. The comment law. Code explains itself through names, small functions, and control flow a reader
   follows in one pass. A low comment count is the first signal of good code. Comments are allowed
   at the top of a file and directly above a declaration, and only in the language's multi-line doc
   syntax (/** */ for TSDoc and JSDoc, """...""" for Python, the // block above the declaration for
   Go, /// for Rust). No inline comments, ever. No comments inside a function body: a step that
   needs narrating is a function that needs extracting. No narration, no journal, no TODO, no
   commented-out code, no author or date or ticket metadata. A doc comment carries the contract, the
   units, the failure mode, or the non-obvious why; if it restates the signature, delete it. The one
   exception, a constraint the code cannot express or a ceiling marker, goes on its own line above
   the code, never trailing. A trailing comment blocks the edit.

2. The laziness ladder. Before writing code: does it need to exist, does this repo already do it,
   does the standard library do it, does the platform do it, does an installed dependency do it, can
   it be one line. Only then write the minimum that works.

Full text in rules/core.md. The named smells you will be reviewed against, and should cite when you
report, are in rules/clean-code.md: names (N1-N7), functions (F1-F4), general (G1-G36), tests
(T1-T9). Read it before you write code, along with the stack overlay in rules/stacks/.
CONTEXT

jq -cn --arg c "$context" \
  '{hookSpecificOutput:{hookEventName:"SubagentStart",additionalContext:$c}}'
exit 0
